          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Dogs predominantly signify unclean, contemptible, wicked, or covenant-excluded persons, with context-specific applications to Gentiles, false teachers, apostates, persecutors, and enemies.\",\"book_summary\":\"Dogs signify Gentile political kingdoms or their citizens, especially Rome and Roman soldiers in the crucifixion pattern.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Profane, wicked, or hostile outsiders to the holy community.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Gentile political powers or their citizens, especially Rome.\",\"core_relation\":\"COMPATIBLE_OVERLAP\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book selects Gentile political identity—particularly Roman power and soldiers—as the controlling referent and extends the image to kingdoms themselves. Consensus allows Gentiles and hostile persecutors as applications but does not make political kingdoms the general meaning and also includes nonpolitical cases such as false teachers, apostates, and the wicked generally.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction in identifying the Canaanite woman or Rome’s soldiers with covenant outsiders or hostile enemies. The material difference is that the book reclassifies the symbol primarily in political-national terms and thereby excludes or sidelines several standard moral and religious applications.\",\"rationale\":\"The Roman/Gentile reading falls within recognized consensus applications of dogs to Gentile outsiders and persecuting enemies, so it is not wholly novel or core-contradictory. Nevertheless, identifying dogs as wild-beast kingdoms or citizens is a substantive narrowing and reclassification rather than merely alternate wording.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Matthew 15 and Mark 7 support association with a Gentile outsider, and Psalm 22 supports hostile persecutors, but Matthew 27 never calls the Roman soldiers dogs. The broader claim that a dog represents a kingdom is not directly established by the printed passages; Revelation 22:15 instead places dogs among wicked persons generally.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
